WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.875
Estimation deliberately replaces awkward quantities with nearby convenient ones

00:00:03.875 --> 00:00:14.300
One hundred ninety eight is close to two hundred, and four hundred three is close to four hundred, so their sum should be close to six hundred.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.264
The exact sum is six hundred one

00:00:18.264 --> 00:00:29.300
Rounding the first number up by two and the second down by three changes the total by negative one, explaining why the estimate of six hundred is slightly low.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.636
For multiplication, nearby round factors often give a useful scale

00:00:34.636 --> 00:00:39.479
Nineteen groups of thirty one should be near twenty groups of thirty

00:00:39.479 --> 00:00:45.533
An answer around sixty or six thousand would have the wrong size before detailed checking.

00:00:46.833 --> 00:00:50.047
When an estimate must be justified, use bounds

00:00:50.047 --> 00:00:57.277
Replacing both positive factors by smaller numbers gives a lower bound, while larger factors give an upper bound

00:00:57.277 --> 00:01:02.900
Here the true product lies strictly between five hundred forty and six hundred forty.
